# Architecture Improvements

Implement only after they become necessary.

- Docker
- CI/CD
- S3 image storage
- Recommendation engine
- Background job queue
- Analytics dashboard
- Caching layer

What I would do in your position
Finish the MVP locally.
Deploy React to Vercel.
Dockerize FastAPI.
Deploy that container to AWS App Runner.
Eventually move PostgreSQL to RDS.
Add GitHub Actions to run Pytest before deployment.

Distributed systems: Figma's multiplayer system keeps many users editing the same document synchronized through persistent client/server connections. You have to think about concurrent edits, ordering, conflicts, reconnecting clients, and consistency.
Databases and storage: deciding how data is modeled, partitioned, queried, replicated, migrated, and scaled—not merely calling PostgreSQL from an endpoint. Figma's recent infrastructure work includes building systems around growing Postgres workloads.
Caching: determining what should live in memory/Redis-like systems so requests don't constantly hit databases. Figma recently built a new internal caching platform specifically around scalability and reliability.
Performance: figuring out why something takes 500 ms instead of 100 ms, reducing unnecessary data transfer, optimizing queries, and changing client/server interactions. Figma has described infrastructure work specifically aimed at reducing interaction latency.
Reliability: handling server crashes, network failures, retries, failover, data loss prevention, monitoring, and incident response. Their multiplayer infrastructure, for example, added a write-ahead log to improve durability.
Infrastructure/platform: containers, Kubernetes, deployment systems, load balancing, service discovery, observability, CI/CD, AWS resources, and tooling that allows other engineers to deploy safely.
Data pipelines: moving and processing huge quantities of data reliably; Figma has discussed rebuilding pipelines to move from multi-day latency toward near-real-time results.